{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability as he inherited the county while still a minor. His mother acted as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was raised in the court of Jerusalem and was closely tied to the royal family. His upbringing prepared him for the complex diplomatic and military challenges that defined his rule. He was known for his intelligence and his deep understanding of the political landscape of the Levant.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III faced constant threats from Muslim rulers especially Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. He was a key player in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and often served as regent when the king was absent or too young to rule. He was appointed regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond's leadership during this time was controversial. Some nobles distrusted him because of his cautious approach to warfare. He preferred diplomacy over direct confrontation which earned him both allies and enemies. His alliance with the Assassins and his negotiations with Muslim leaders were seen by some as betrayal but were strategic moves to preserve Christian territories.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who warned against the rash campaign led by Guy of Lusignan. He refused to join the crusader army at first citing lack of preparation and poor terrain. His hesitation was interpreted as disloyalty. When the battle ended in disaster Raymond was captured by Saladin. He was held prisoner for several months before being released. After his release he returned to Tripoli and died in 1187. His death marked the end of an era of cautious diplomacy in the Crusader states. His legacy is debated among historians with some praising his pragmatism and others condemning his reluctance to fight. He left behind a county that was vulnerable and soon fell to Muslim forces after his death.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.001701099332422018}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.0006493605324067175}, 